From: szabo@netcom.com (Nick Szabo)
Date: Sat, 8 Oct 1994 05:08:12 -0700 (PDT)
Subject: [#94-10-138] M.S. in Studies of the Future from UHCL

Fred Moulton: 
> I think that Nick overstates the case somewhat about futurists.  

I was discussing capital-F Futurists, as in the kind that run
the magazine "The Futurist".  I was contrasting it to the 
Extropian brand of futurism, which I think we largely share.  
There are surely a fair number of futurists with intermediate 
positions, as well, including many contemporary SF authors.
Most small-f futurism during this century has, following
Welles, been biased towards Fabian socialism, in its basic
view of the world if not explicitly.  The contemporary
landscape is a bit more balanced (although spending my time
mostly with futurists of an Extropian bent may have biased
my feel for the overall proportions).  Big-F Futurists 
retain most of the socialist memes, such as their obsession with 
grand Plans for "society" and large preplanned communal habitats,
ignorance of economics, ignorance of the wide variety of individual 
needs, active ignorance and criticism of technology that is important 
to individuals rather than "society" (such as advanced forms
of self-transformation and life extension), venomous diatribes 
against "short sighted businessmen" for failing to fund their 
naive Plans, incessent politicking for forced funding of their 
Plans, ad nausuem.

Futurists fell into the trap of thinking that the future 
has far fewer possibilities than it really does,
and so they completely miss the future that really does
come about.  They wasted everybody's time focussing
on a future that was only infinitesimally likely to ever
have happened (because there were so many other possible
futures), and would not have been more important than many
of the alternatives even if it did happen.  They obsessed on
single variables ("world population", "cost per pound
to orbit", etc.) and ignored the huge number of other variables
in the world that are just as important, including variables
closer to home that they could actually have had a chance of
understanding and improving.  Going further, many Futurists have 
turned their visions into religious dogma and government coercion 
which actively prevented the exploration of other alternatives
by the rest of us.  A pox on their kind, I say.

Nick Szabo				szabo@netcom.com

