Subject: [#94-10-148] 2CATCH A CAT

    Many pet stores and animal control agencies stock "Have-A-Heart"
(or similar brand) live animal traps, which I have used to recapture
an escaped cat, and also to capture strays that I wished to make
household pets of.  These traps are wire-frame elongated cages with a
spring-loaded double door at one end, activated by disturbance of a
trigger plate at the other, closed end.  You put a dish of food on the
trigger plate, and set the trap in a likely place with newspaper on
the wire floor -- or something like that -- so that the surface will
not feel weird and encourage suspicion.

    Evidence of the innocuous perception of these traps by the trapped
animals is that when I used one to recapture my escaped feral stray,
Wraith, I logged about 20 captures of neighborhood pets and strays
before I finally got the cat I wanted, and some of those 20 were
multiple captures of the same animal.  The cats seemed to regard the
experience as a free meal in a private dining room, with a nice butler
to open the door when they were finished.

    And the two parking-lot strays that I captured to take home entered
the trap within an hour or two of setting it up, at a place where I had
already been leaving food for them.

