Subject: [#94-10-150] Ritalin and ADD

Ray writes:
> 
>   Which reminds me, anyone know where to get Ritalin? Anyone here
> got any positive benefits from it (assuming you were diagnosed/self-diagnosed
> yourself as having attention deficit disorder)?
> 

Without a prescription I know of no domestic sources of Ritalin.  I've heard
of mail-order suppliers, but nothing outside the US.  Ritalin is considered
a controlled substance and it's manufacture and distribution are heavily
regulated -- I can't get more than a 30-day supply per prescription, and
shortages often develop towards the end of the year as the government-set
supplies are sold out.

Being ADD (sans hyperactivity) and learning disabled, Ritalin has helped
me tremendously.  Mainly by making it easier for me to concentrate and focus
on what I'm doing and to help me filter out distractions.  Ritalin also
has some anti-depressive effects.  The most notable drawback is that the
effects last only three hours for me, and then I'm back to my abnormal self.

Ritalin does nothing for my learning disabilities.  These affect my working and
short-term memory and my abilities with expressive communications.  So while
I can now read a paragraph without distraction, I will remember little of
what I read and can't articulate what I do retain.

Obviously lurking comes quite naturally to me.

There's a lot more to this.  Feel free to ask.

